cff-version: 1.2.0
title: >-
  Three mechanisms inflate reported performance on a widely used public
  CKD benchmark: a leakage-controlled, case-mix-aware re-analysis
message: >-
  If you use this analysis, its code, or its provenance findings, please
  cite it. If you use the underlying dataset, cite the UCI releases listed
  under references below - and note this work's finding that the two
  releases share their patients record-for-record.
type: software
authors:
  - name: "Repository author"
    note: >-
      Replace with author name, affiliation and ORCID before archiving or
      submission. Left unfilled deliberately rather than guessed.
abstract: >-
  A re-analysis of the UCI "Risk Factor Prediction of Chronic Kidney
  Disease" dataset asking why machine-learning results on it cluster at
  the discrimination ceiling. Three mechanisms are identified and
  quantified: target leakage from outcome-derived columns; a case mix in
  which 84% of cases carry stage 3-5 disease, so haemoglobin alone reaches
  ROC-AUC 0.968; and non-independent validation cohorts - a record-level
  provenance check finds all 200 patients present in the 2015 UCI CKD
  release, so published cross-dataset validations between the two evaluate
  models on their own training population. Additional findings: the
  published interval encoding destroys 0.27 ROC-AUC of univariate signal
  in serum creatinine without changing any multivariable conclusion, and
  the release supplied constant "normal" values for missing laboratory
  measurements whose absence is strongly outcome-associated.
keywords:
  - chronic kidney disease
  - data leakage
  - spectrum bias
  - case mix
  - external validation
  - benchmark quality
  - clinical prediction models
  - reproducibility
license: MIT
repository-code: "https://example.invalid/replace-before-archiving"
